.\" Template for archivectl.1: scripts/gen-docs.py fills in the marked parts
.\" from the dispatcher, libexec/archivectl/*.sh and lib/*.sh, reading them only.
.TH ARCHIVECTL 1 2026-11-15 "archivectl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
archivectl \- encrypted, compressed BorgBackup archives of a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.\" @generated synopsis
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B archivectl
keeps one Borg repository per backed-up directory, on this host or on
another one.
.B init
marks a directory as a backup source by writing a
.I .borgbackup
file into it, see
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5),
and initialises its repository, encrypted with
.BR repokey ,
under
.BR $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT ,
named after the directory's path: the leading
.B /
left out, every other
.B /
written
.BR __ ,
so that
.I /home/adhd/test
is kept in
.IR home__adhd__test .
So that no two directories share a name,
.B %
is written
.BR %25 ,
a newline
.BR %0A ,
an underscore that begins or ends a part of the path or stands next to
another one
.BR %5F ,
a
.B !
that begins the name
.BR %21 ,
and
.B /
itself is
.BR %2F .
A name longer than 199 bytes is cut to its first 135 bytes, followed by
.B ~
and the SHA\-256 of the path, 200 bytes in all, which no shorter name can
equal; the log names below are made the same way.
A repository that exists under that name is used again, and so is one
under the name earlier versions gave, the whole path with
.B %
written
.BR %25 ,
.B /
.B %2F
and a newline
.BR %0A .
dedupctl named it with every
.B /
turned into
.B !
and every
.B !
doubled, which two directories can share: when a repository under that
name exists and under neither other one,
.B init
refuses and says so.
.PP
Every other command looks for the nearest
.I .borgbackup
in the current directory, or the directory
.B \-C
names, or their parents, changes into that directory, and reads the
file.
An archive is named
.IR <N>_<YYYY\-MM\-DD_HHMMSS> ,
.I N
being its number and the rest the time it was made.
The number is the id every command prints and takes: 1 for the first
archive, then one more for each, never given twice, also after a delete;
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<name>.sequence
holds the last one given, since a repository on another host has no
room for it.
A command takes the full name too.
Earlier versions named an archive
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH:MM:SS ;
such archives, and any other not named with a number, are listed with
the numbers after the last one given, in time order, and the first
.B create
or
.B delete
renames each with
.B borg rename
to the name with its number and says so on standard error and in the
log; with
.B \-n
they report the renames and do none.
.PP
Selecting and acting are separate commands:
.B list
prints records, one per line, its fields separated by tabs and the
number first, and
.B delete
acts on the records it reads from standard input, or on the numbers it
is given.
Standard output carries records only: those of
.BR status ,
.B list
and
.BR diff ,
and the record of the archive
.B create
made.
An empty answer prints nothing.
In a path of a record, a backslash, tab or newline is written
.BR \e\e ,
.B \et
or
.BR \en .
Messages, progress, summaries and the output of
.B borg
go to standard error.
An id given on the command line must name an archive, or the command
ends with status 1 before it runs borg on it.
Every command that writes,
.BR init ,
.BR create ,
.B delete
and
.BR restore ,
takes
.BR \-n ,
which reports on standard error what it would do and writes nothing.
.PP
Before the command, the dispatcher takes
.B \-C
.I dir
and
.BR \-h ;
every other option goes after the command.
.B archivectl
with no command prints the record of
.B status
and then the help, which fits one screen;
.B archivectl
.I command
.B \-\-help
prints the help of one command.
Every command refuses an option it does not know with status 2 before it
does anything.
.SS "The passphrase"
.B init
reads the passphrase of a new repository once, from the terminal, or
takes it from
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ,
keeps it in
.BR pass (1)
as
.IR archivectl/<name> ,
and writes
.B BORG_PASSCOMMAND="pass show archivectl/<name>"
into
.IR .borgbackup ;
every command exports it for its borg calls, so that gpg-agent answers
and nothing is typed.
Only with
.B \-\-no\-pass
or without
.B pass
installed does
.B init
leave it out; borg then asks for the passphrase itself.
For a source initialised before this,
.B init \-\-store\-passphrase
reads the passphrase once, checks it with
.BR "borg info" ,
keeps it in pass and adds the
.B BORG_PASSCOMMAND
line to its
.IR .borgbackup .
.PP
With neither
.B BORG_PASSPHRASE
nor
.B BORG_PASSCOMMAND
set and an encrypted repository, a command reads the passphrase once,
from the terminal, and hands it to each of its borg calls in their
environment only; it is never written anywhere or logged.
Without a terminal on standard error it ends with status 1 instead of
asking.
.SS "Remote repositories"
.B init \-\-repo
.I url
uses the repository at
.I url
as given: an
.B ssh://user@host[:port]/path
URL, an scp-style
.BR user@host:path ,
or a local path, as for BorgBase:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl init \-\-repo ssh://xxxx@xxxx.repo.borgbase.com/./repo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-repo ,
a
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
that is such a URL means
.I <root>/<name>
on that host.
For a repository on another host borg itself answers whether it exists,
with
.BR "borg info" ;
.B init
initialises it with
.B borg init \-\-make\-parent\-dirs
or uses the one that is there.
borg reaches the host with ssh, or the command
.B BORG_RSH
names, from the environment or
.IR .borgbackup ,
for instance
.BR "BORG_RSH='ssh \-i ~/.ssh/borgbase_ed25519'" ;
.B archivectl
asks for nothing beyond what borg and ssh ask.
.SH OPTIONS
.\" @generated options
.SH COMMANDS
.\" @generated commands
.\" @prose create
.PP
The archive is written with
.B borg create \-\-compression lz4 \-\-stats \-\-list \-\-filter=CE
from
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
leaving out the
.B EXCLUDES
of
.IR .borgbackup .
The create takes the next number and keeps it in the sequence file,
holding a lock on it meanwhile, so that two creates never share a
number; a number whose archive failed is not given again either.
Of borg's output, standard error gets what borg warns about, and
.B \-\-stats
adds borg's statistics; otherwise one line says the archive was
created.
borg runs with
.BR BORG_EXIT_CODES=modern ,
and its warnings, status 1 or 100 to 127, alone or together, keep the
archive: a file borg could not read is left out of it, and one that
changed while borg read it is stored as read.
borg names each such file on standard error, with
.B E
or
.B C
in front, and
.B create
writes those names to the log, prints the record and ends with status 0.
borg writes its output with
.BR \-\-log\-json ,
shown on standard error as text, so that these file names are never
taken from its messages.
The status is borg's own, not that of a command around it.
On a borg error the archive is deleted only if this run's borg committed
it, which the fingerprint it printed shows, and
.B create
ends with borg's status, after the parent below; a
.B SOURCE_DIR
that is no directory or cannot be entered and read ends it with status 1,
also after the parent and also in the log; borg does not run then.
A file borg names that way is logged with its own bytes, which borg keeps
in its warning about the file when it shows a byte that is not UTF\-8 as
.BR ? .
.B \-n
runs
.B borg create \-\-dry\-run \-\-list
with its list on standard error and writes no log.
.PP
When a directory above the repository root has a
.I .borgbackup
of its own, an archive is then created there too, with the same options:
a
.B \-n
stays a dry run in the parent.
The parent's record goes to standard error, so that standard output
holds the record of this repository's archive only.
The parent's archive is created whatever became of this one; the status
is this archive's, or the parent's when this one was made.
.\" @end
.\" @prose status
.PP
The number and the date of the newest are empty when there is no
archive; the size is the repository's deduplicated, compressed size, as
.B borg info
gives it.
.\" @end
.\" @prose list
.PP
The date is the archive's start time as borg records it,
.IR "YYYY\-MM\-DD HH:MM:SS" ,
and the size its original size from
.BR "borg info" .
The options combine: the age options select first, then
.B \-\-first
and
.B \-\-last
take from what is left, in that order.
.\" @end
.\" @prose delete
.PP
Ids given as arguments are deleted; without them,
.B delete
reads standard input, where the first field of each line is the id, the
rest of the line ignored and blank lines skipped.
Ids as arguments while standard input is a pipe or a file are refused
with status 2, so that no input is half ignored; with neither, a
terminal on standard input,
.B delete
waits for no typed ids: it says how it is used, as in
.BR "archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete \-n" ,
and ends with status 2.
.PP
Every id, a number or a full name, must name an archive, or nothing is
deleted and the status is 1.
The archives are removed with one
.BR "borg delete" ;
the space they held is freed by
.BR "borg compact" .
Their numbers are not given again.
.\" @end
.\" @prose restore
.PP
The archive is extracted with
.B borg extract
into
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
the repository root with the configuration
.B init
writes, or into the directory of
.BR \-\-to ,
created when missing.
A
.I path
is a path in the archive, relative to the source, and everything below
it is extracted.
.B \-\-mount
mounts the archive, or the paths in it, with
.BR "borg mount" ,
which needs FUSE; unmount it with
.B borg umount
.IR dir .
.B \-n
runs
.B borg extract \-\-dry\-run \-\-list
with its list on standard error, and with
.B \-\-mount
only checks that the archive exists.
.\" @end
.\" @prose diff
.PP
.B added
is a path only the second holds,
.B removed
a path only the first holds, and
.B modified
a path whose content or type differs, also one that is a file in one
and a directory or a link in the other.
Two archives are compared with
.BR "borg diff \-\-json\-lines" ,
which compares the chunks borg stored and reads no file data; changes of
times, owners and permissions alone are left out.
borg shows a byte that is not UTF\-8 as
.BR ? ;
a path holding
.B ?
is matched to its bytes in the listings of the two archives, which
.B borg list
writes with
.B PYTHONIOENCODING=unicode_escape
without loss: a path in the second archive only is added, one in the first
only removed, and one in both whose type, link target or content
.RB ( "borg list {sha256}" )
differs is modified.
borg lists a hard link to an earlier item with that item's path and no
content, so a hard link's content is the item's it links to.
A failing
.B borg list
there ends the run with borg's status.
When borg's records and the paths found that way are not as many, the
comparison is refused with status 1; one with
.B \-\-scratch
still works.
The live source is compared by extracting the archive into a directory
made under the one
.B \-\-scratch
names, which needs room for the whole archive and is removed afterwards,
and comparing the trees by content with
.B rsync \-\-dry\-run \-\-checksum \-\-delete
leaving out the
.BR EXCLUDES .
Without
.BR \-\-scratch ,
that comparison is refused with status 2, and so is a
.B \-\-scratch
inside the source, whose extraction would count as added.
.\" @end
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, also
.BR \-h " / " \-\-help
after any command or none,
.B init
on a directory that has a
.I .borgbackup
already, and
.B delete
with no input.
.TP
.B 1
An unknown command (the help goes to standard error), no
.I .borgbackup
was found in the current directory or its parents, also for
.B archivectl
with no command, an id names no archive, the repository needs a
passphrase and there is no terminal to ask on,
.B init
was given a wrong passphrase for a repository there already, or pass
holds the entry it would write,
.B create
found no
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
.B diff
of two archives could not tell paths borg shows alike apart,
.B init
found a repository under the old name, or another error.
A failing
.B borg
command, a
.B borg list
that checks a name among them, ends the run with its own status; in
.BR create ,
a warning does not.
.TP
.B 2
An option the command does not know or without its value, a
.B \-C
directory that cannot be entered,
.B delete
given ids and piped input both, or neither,
.B init
given more than one path or a path that is not a directory,
.B restore
given no id or both
.B \-\-to
and
.BR \-\-mount ,
or
.B diff
given no id, more than two, or one without
.B \-\-scratch
or with one inside the source.
.TP
.B 3
.I .borgbackup
does not set a variable the command needs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
Where
.B init
creates repositories, a local directory or an
.B ssh://
or scp-style URL; default
.I /data/backup/borg
when
.I /data
is a mount point, else
.IR ~/.borg_backups .
.TP
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ", " BORG_PASSCOMMAND
The passphrase, or the command that prints it, for borg; see
.B The passphrase
above.
.TP
.B BORG_RSH
The command borg reaches another host with, instead of ssh.
.TP
.B PASSWORD_STORE_DIR
Where
.B pass
keeps its entries; default
.IR ~/.password\-store .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the logs are kept; default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .borgbackup
The backup source's configuration, in the directory it backs up; see
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5).
.TP
.I $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name>/
The repository
.B init
creates.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<repository>.sequence
The last number given to an archive of a repository,
.I <repository>
being its location turned into a file name as for the repository.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/archivectl/<name>.log
The log of a repository,
.I <name>
being its root's path turned into a file name as for the repository.
Every command that writes appends a line with the time per thing it
did.
The output of borg stays out of it, since borg can show the passphrase
there, and the log and its directory are readable by the user only.
A dry run writes no log.
Logs of earlier versions, one per archive in
.IR $BORG_REPO/logs/ ,
are left as they are.
.SH EXAMPLES
Back up the current directory, and see what is there:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl init && archivectl create
archivectl status
.EE
.RE
.PP
Back up to BorgBase with its own key:
.PP
.RS
.EX
export BORG_RSH='ssh \-i ~/.ssh/borgbase_ed25519'
archivectl init \-\-repo ssh://xxxx@xxxx.repo.borgbase.com/./repo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Delete the archives older than 30 days, after looking at what goes, and
then archive 4:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete \-n
archivectl list \-\-older 30 | archivectl delete
archivectl delete 4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Extract notes.txt from the newest archive into /tmp/old:
.PP
.RS
.EX
archivectl restore \-\-to /tmp/old "$(archivectl list \-\-last 1 | cut \-f1)" notes.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR archivectl.borgbackup (5),
.BR borg (1),
.BR pass (1),
.BR snapshotctl (1)
